```

--------------------------------

### Postman Test Script

Source: https://learning.postman.com/api-docs/api-reference/collections/get-collection.md

Example of a test script used to validate that the response status code is 200.

```javascript
pm.test("Status code is 200", function () {
    pm.response.to.have.status(200);
});
```

--------------------------------

### Test Response Messages Against JSON Schema

Source: https://learning.postman.com/docs/tests-and-scripts/write-scripts/test-examples.md

Asserts that all response messages conform to a specified JSON Schema. This is crucial for validating data structure and types in complex responses.

```javascript
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    username: {
      type: "string",
      pattern: "^[a-z0-9_-]{3,16}$"
    }
  }
};

pm.test('All response messages have correct username', () => {
  pm.response.messages.to.have.jsonSchema(schema);
});
```

### Write pre-request scripts to add dynamic behavior in Postman

Source: https://learning.postman.com/docs/tests-and-scripts/write-scripts/pre-request-scripts.md

Pre-request scripts in Postman allow you to run JavaScript code before a request is sent. By adding code to the Pre-request tab of a request, collection, or folder, you can perform pre-processing tasks like setting variable values, parameters, headers, and body data. These scripts are also useful for debugging by logging output to the Postman Console.

--------------------------------

### Reference Postman responses in scripts > Validate response data with a JSON Schema

Source: https://learning.postman.com/docs/tests-and-scripts/write-scripts/postman-sandbox-reference/pm-response.md

You can write a test assertion using pm.response and Chai Assertion Library BDD syntax to validate response data against a defined JSON Schema. The test fails if the response data is missing required properties or has incorrect data types.

